<title>CANADA: Canadian foreign reserves RISE in February. [CORRECTED 16:49 GMT]</title>
<headline>Canadian foreign reserves RISE in February. [CORRECTED 16:49 GMT]</headline>
<dateline>OTTAWA 1997-03-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>(Corrects headline and first paragraph to show a rise rather than a fall in reserves)</p>
<p>Canadian foreign reserve holdings rose in February, the Finance Department said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>   RESERVES			  Feb 97	Jan 97</p>
<p>   Total			   21.044 bln  20.889 bln</p>
<p>   U.S. dollars		  18.110 bln  17.942 bln</p>
<p>   Other foreign currencies     505 million     508 million</p>
<p>   Gold				 150 million     151 million</p>
<p>   Special drawing rights     1.132 bln   1.133 bln</p>
<p>   IMF reserves		   1.147 bln   1.155 bln</p>
<p>Note - Figures in U.S. dollars.  </p>
<p>Official government operations increased reserves by US$326 million.</p>
<p>Details of the changes in the month:</p>
<p>    Gain on gold sales		   -</p>
<p>    Earnings on investments	    +93</p>
<p>    Revaluation effects		  -17</p>
<p>    Foreign currency debt	     +263</p>
<p>    Other transactions		    -9</p>
<p>NOTE - in millions of U.S. dollars  </p>
<p>During February, Canada bills increased by $163.1 million to a level of outstanding bills of $5.194 billion. Sales of Canada notes totalled $100 million, increasing the level of outstanding notes to $1.640 billion.</p>
<p>((Reuters Ottawa Bureau (613) 235-6745))</p>
